DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iioo provider allows the tracing of disk I/O events.  The iioo::::::ssttaarrtt()
     probe fires when a I/O request is about to be sent to the backing driver
     of a disk(9) object.  This occurs after all GEOM(4) transformations have
     been performed on the request.  The iioo::::::ddoonnee() probe fires when a I/O
     request is completed.  Both probes take a _s_t_r_u_c_t _b_i_o _* representing the
     I/O request as their first argument.  The second argument is a _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _d_e_v_s_t_a_t _* for the underlying disk(9) object.

AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS
     The fields of _s_t_r_u_c_t _b_i_o are described in the g_bio(9) manual page, and
     the fields of _s_t_r_u_c_t _d_e_v_s_t_a_t are described in the devstat(9) manual page.
     Translators for the _b_u_f_i_n_f_o___t and _d_e_v_i_n_f_o___t D types are defined in
     _/_u_s_r_/_l_i_b_/_d_t_r_a_c_e_/_i_o_._d.

FFIILLEESS
     /usr/lib/dtrace/io.d  DTrace type and translator definitions for the iioo
                           provider.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following script shows a per-process breakdown of total I/O by disk
     device:

           #pragma D option quiet

           io:::start
           {
                   @[args[1]->device_name, execname, pid] = sum(args[0]->bio_bcount);
           }

           END
           {
                   printf("%10s %20s %10s %15s\n", "DEVICE", "APP", "PID", "BYTES");
                   printa("%10s %20s %10d %15@d\n", @);
           }

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     This provider is not compatible with the iioo provider found in Solaris, as
     its probes use native FreeBSD argument types.

